hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well one of the  perennial problems dealing with  friendship that Marcus Tullius Cicero  talks about in his work on friendship is  this dynamic and perhaps even for many  people dilemma should you favor old  friends or should you favor new friends  and he doesn't talk at great length  about this but it's it's worth  reflecting why this is viewed as a  problem in the first place and it was  just as much of a problem in Cicero's  own day and time as it remains for us  and time is is really part of the  problem I mean we we think about old and  new those are temporal words right the  friends who you've had for a very long  time perhaps you met in childhood or in  school together went through some sort  of thing they could be family members  who you you know been in touch with your  entire life right and then we've got new  friends something new is happening a  friendship a locus of affection and  goodwill an agreement is blossoming into  a relationship and that's happening now  or you know just happened in the near  past compared to the friendships that  you've had that grew in the past  have been going along for quite some  time so we've got that sort of sense but  I also mean another sense in which time  is a major factor friendship is a  challenge in part because time is  limited for all of us we all get exactly  the same 24 hours as everybody else and  we can find ways to you know multiply  our time or trying to save our time all  sorts of life hacks and management  things but you really when it comes down  to it you still only get those 24 hours  and you hopefully spend about you know  seven to eight of them sleeping so you  know there's all sorts of other things  do you have enough time to do justice to  both of these kinds of friendships the  new friendships which you know demand  quite a bit of time upfront because you  don't actually know the person yet and  you're in that phase of learning about  them and then learning about you and  doing a lot of things together or do you  devote your time to the old friendships  that do require maintenance you can't  rely on them as just sort of you know  I'll go back to it every couple years  and we'll still be friends they may have  completely changed if you've lost touch  like that or they may not have you know  it's kind of a risk so we're do you a  lot this valuable resource that you have  of time and not just time thought  affection attention so Cicero raises  this as a genuine problem should new  friends and notice that he's got this  provision here worthy of friendship not  so it's not just anybody that you claim  to be a friend or calls you a friend but  people who are actually worthy of  friendship should they be preferred to  old friends we can make this very  concrete right you have a particular day  that you have free and two people call  you up and they say hey I'm going to  have a party can you come over we'd  really like to have you here one of them  is somebody who you're in a new  relationship with that's grown  into friendship you think they're a good  person and they certainly treat you like  a good person the other is somebody who  you've known for a long time you know  that they're a good person you like them  who do you choose and do you if you're  going to say something to the friend do  you do you tell them sorry I can't go to  your party new friend because I've  already got this old friend over here  and I need to spend time with them  or do you say listen you know we can  always see each other I've got to spend  time with this person you don't mind do  you  these are the sorts of things that do  come up quite a bit in in friendships  and it can happen in you know regular  friendships that were all experienced in  it can happen in virtual relationships  as well so he point he raises this  question right and then he adds an  interesting little metaphor as we're  accustom to prefer young horses to old  ones now not too many of us have horses  right so it's a little hard to relate to  that but think about this as you prefer  your new smartphone to your old  smartphone or laptop or desktop or car  or I don't know tie alright shirt  anything any object that you might say  or if we're thinking about animals do  you prefer the the new puppy the new  kitten to your old aged dog or cat who  can't play anymore  now it gets really personal doesn't it  right and so this this young horse is  two old horses why would we prefer young  horses well the young horses you know  can can do their work better this is  kind of an instrumental way of looking  at friendship and there's also it does  capture the idea that there there's  perhaps more excitement on the side of  the new friend  as an object of our affection than there  is on the side of the old true  reliable friend who he might be passing  up so Cicero says no no no we should not  do that  and he gives several reasons why one of  them is really quite interesting he says  there should be in this translation has  no surfed it we could translate that as  surplus or overabundance or anything  like that  Sathya toss is is the word there and  that's the word that we get satiety from  when we talk about you know eating  enough food and our stomach is full and  we feel that sense of having had enough  there is just from saying there isn't  that had enough of friendship as a  matter of fact we should try to do both  at the same time no he doesn't tell us  how to juggle this but he does tell us  that we should keep our old friendships  and he suggests that we can do that  through habit we'll look at that a  little bit more and what he has to say  about that in just a bit but then he  also says that we shouldn't reject new  friendships if they offer a certain kind  of hope right they says new friendships  are not to be scorned if they offer the  hope of bearing fruit like green shoots  of corn that do not disappoint us at  harvest time many of us probably are not  gardeners I myself have grown a lot of  things in my life when he when we're  translating this is corn it doesn't  necessarily mean corn in the sense that  we Americans talk about it he's talking  about wheat or barley or anything like  that I didn't meant as a seed when we  see these these shoots arising that's  sort of like seeing a friendship that  has promise the promise of bearing fruit  that is enjoyment and the sort of  intimacy that becomes possible in  genuine friendships when we think that  that's there  we shouldn't scorn that but he says the  old friendships must preserve their own  place for the force of age and habit is  very great and he also says something  quite interesting about the horse  metaphor so if you thought that Cicero  was being a bit inhumane about horses at  this point he says even in the case of  the horse just now referred to everybody  no nothing preventing would rather use  one to which he has grown accustomed  than one that is untrained and new and  this habit is strong in the case not  only of animate but also inanimate  things since we delight even in places  the rugged and wild in which we've lived  for a fairly long time people will say  this about their hometown and somebody  else complains about it they're like you  know Milwaukee sucks and they hey who  are you to talk about that that's my  City sort of like when people complain  about our family members only we get to  say bad things about them right because  we have this familiarity we have this  this sense of affection towards them  that's built up through habit so Cicero  thinks that in some sense if we if we  work at it we can have it all that  doesn't mean that we can automatically  be friends with everybody and if we're  gonna follow out this agricultural  metaphor it's not like every single  possible shoot that's coming up we  should be like oh this is a great friend  a great friend a great friend no no we  shouldn't be doing that we would still  have to be rather selective and here  again we come to the the factor of time  he says two very interesting things  about old friendships that help to you  know stress why it is that we need to  hold on to them to inducements and both  of these do have to do with time one of  them well actually both of them are  metaphors so the first one is that old  friendships are like wine that has been  aged and because of that aging process  qualities of the wine come out that were  not immediately apparent in the earlier  stages of that wine and it becomes as he  says more pleasant more delightful so  old friendships in which this intimacy  has developed have a greater depth to  them a better bouquet right although  maybe our friends don't always smell  great it really depends on the friends  buzz of it but but you get the idea and  we could actually follow out this this  you know vintners metaphor a little bit  further the young friends are very  attractive because they're like that  fresh  young wine if you've ever had you know  for example in Portugal they have what  can be translated as green wine that's  very young it's kind of effervescent  it's got a bold you know taste to it or  if you've ever had like Nouveau  Beaujolais right you know these these  things can be quite nice but it's not  the same thing as the really well aged  Cabernet isn't which has a greater  potential you might say to to to please  so that's an interesting metaphor right  there and then he takes from Aristotle  who took it from other people as well  it's a proverb already old and  Aristotle's time that we have to eat  some measures of salt with a person  before we can actually consider them to  be a true friend and the idea here is we  need to develop intimacy one way in  which we do develop intimacy is by  living with and having meals with a  person we converse over the meals we  share the salt you know iron if  somebody's hoarding salt which was a  valuable commodity I mean I'm tell you  don't want to be friends with them right  there's stingy with the salt right or  maybe they they salt everything too much  but having that that bucket or measure  of salt together allows us to to know  that somebody really is a good friend so  what is what is Cicero's answer to this  there's good reasons to want to have old  and new friends we shouldn't just  withdraw into old friendships and be  closed off to the potentials of new  friendships at the same time we have to  keep our old friends we really ought to  do this and notice that he's not making  an argument here based on sort of duty  or obligation he's saying it's it's  better for you to keep your old  friendships that's the smart thing to do  so how is this done he doesn't actually  tell us but he is providing us with his  own viewpoint on the matter  you
 

